🚽 Test case: issue #389 -- the array forms that still do NOT decay, each
🚽 refused with a message naming what to write instead.
🚽
🚽 1. A 2-D array. In C it decays to a pointer to its first ROW
🚽    (`rizz (*)[2]`), a type this language cannot spell; quietly decaying
🚽    it to `&m[0][0]` would accept code C rejects.
🚽 2. An array whose element type is not the pointer's. Decay makes this
🚽    one keystroke away, and without the check the callee reads and
🚽    writes float bits as ints. The same check applies to an explicit
🚽    `&fs[0]`, which used to be accepted silently too.
🚽    The match covers storage WIDTH and signedness, not just the base
🚽    type (#389 review): `giga rizz *` and `rizz *` are both "pointer to
🚽    int" to a VarType-only check, but `p[1]` on the giga one strides 8
🚽    bytes, so a `rizz xs[2]` passed to it was an ASan heap-buffer-
🚽    overflow. `nonut rizz *` vs `rizz` stays in bounds but reinterprets
🚽    the bits. Both directions of each are refused.
🚽 3. `&xs`. In C that is a pointer to the whole array; here it used to
🚽    evaluate to the interpreter's own slot holding the array's storage
🚽    pointer -- a valid-looking pointer to the wrong thing (`*p` printed
🚽    an unrelated number).
🚽
🚽 An array passed to a NON-pointer parameter is covered by
🚽 array_arg_scalar_param_fail.brainrot.

rizz first(rizz *p) {
    bussin p[0];
}

skibidi set_wide(giga rizz *p) {
    p[1] = 7;
}

skibidi set_unsigned(nonut rizz *p) {
    p[1] = 7;
}

skibidi main {
    rizz m[2][2];
    chad fs[2] = {1.5, 2.5};
    rizz xs[2] = {4, 5};
    giga rizz gs[2] = {4, 5};
    nonut rizz us[2] = {4, 5};

    yapping("%d", first(m));
    yapping("%d", first(fs));
    yapping("%d", first(&fs[0]));
    rizz *p = &xs;
    set_wide(xs);
    set_unsigned(xs);
    yapping("%d %d", first(gs), first(us));
    bussin 0;
}
